712111115455627,-0.10571347922086716,-0.9008256793022156,-0.047669265419244766,-0.014127600938081741,-0.7478064894676208,-0.0965203121304512,-0.002653650939464569,-0.0755828246474266,-0.14245067536830902,-0.001570302527397871,0.0,-0.8664519786834717,-1.2755313036905136e-05,-0.006836476735770702,-3.814689989667386e-06,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.26092809438705444,-5.125986263010418e-06,-2.7179348762729205e-05,-0.020484207198023796,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-7.986990567587782e-06,-0.07810769975185394,-0.00829524826258421,-2.9802276912960224e-06,-0.1031709834933281,-2.312633478140924e-05],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,26313,979,279,47311,198,78045,79,2075,8,284,220,16,488,856,61,17,488,856,61,19,488,856,61,21,488,1124,67816,488,856,47822,17,17,11035,60,285,17779,553,279,47311,400,80,2075,8,284,220,16,488,856,488,856,61,17,488,856,61,18,488,1124,67816,488,856,47822,16,16,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the remainder when the polynomial\n\\[p(x) = 1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + \\dots + x^{22}\\]is divided by the polynomial $q(x) = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \\dots + x^{11}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cfe3669dd6c5d174cd66cdd4f216c5b416b485318f2f4e3e9e26e52d82b848bd"},"seconds":27.309786319732666,"seed":853490024871161258,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of finding the remainder when the polynomial \\( p(x) = 1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + \\dots + x^{22} \\) is divided by the polynomial \\( q(x) = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \\dots + x^{11} \\), we can use properties of geometric series and polynomial division.\n\nFirst, let's express \\( p(x) \\) and \\( q(x) \\) in a more convenient form. The polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is a geometric series with the first term 1 and common ratio \\( x^2 \\), and it has 12 terms:\n\\[ p(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{11} x^{2k} = \\frac{x^{24} - 1}{x^2 - 1} \\]\nThe polynomial \\( q(x) \\) is a geometric series with the first term 1 and common ratio \\( x \\), and it has 12 terms:\n\\[ q(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{11} x^k = \\frac{x^{12} - 1}{x - 1} \\]\n\nWe need to find the remainder when \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( q(x) \\). Let's denote the remainder by \\( r(x) \\). Since the degree of \\( r(x) \\) must be less than the degree of \\( q(x) \\), we can write:\n\\[ p(x) = q(x) \\cdot d(x) + r(x) \\]\nwhere \\( r(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree less than 12.\n\nTo find \\( r(x) \\), we can use the fact that \\( q(x) = \\frac{x^{12} - 1}{x - 1} \\). Notice that \\( q(x) \\) is zero when \\( x \\) is a 12th root of unity other than 1. Let \\( \\omega \\) be a primitive 12th root of unity. Then \\( \\omega^{12} = 1 \\) and \\( q(\\omega) = 0 \\). Since \\( p(\\omega) \\) and \\( q(\\omega) \\) are both zero, we have:\n\\[ p(\\omega) = q(\\omega) \\cdot d(\\omega) + r(\\omega) \\implies p(\\omega) = r(\\omega) \\]\nbecause \\( q(\\omega) = 0 \\).\n\nNow, let's evaluate \\( p(\\omega) \\):\n\\[ p(\\omega) = 1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10} + \\omega^{12} + \\omega^{14} + \\omega^{16} + \\omega^{18} + \\omega^{20} + \\omega^{22} \\]\nSince \\( \\omega^{12} = 1 \\), we can simplify the exponents modulo 12:\n\\[ p(\\omega) = 1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10} + 1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10} \\]\n\\[ p(\\omega) = 2(1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10}) \\]\nThe sum \\( 1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10} \\) is the sum of all 12th roots of unity except 1 and \\(-1\\) (since \\(\\omega^6 = -1\\)), and it is known to be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ 1 + \\omega + \\omega^2 + \\omega^3 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^5 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^7 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^9 + \\omega^{10} + \\omega^{11} = 0 \\]\n\\[ 1 + \\omega^2 + \\omega^4 + \\omega^6 + \\omega^8 + \\omega^{10} = -1 - (\\omega + \\omega^3 + \\omega^5 + \\omega^7 + \\omega^9 + \\omega^{11}) = -1 + 1 = 0 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ p(\\omega) = 2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\]\nSince \\( p(\\omega) = r(\\omega) \\) and \\( r(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree less than 12, and \\( r(x) \\) must be zero for all 12th roots of unity (since \\( p(x) \\) and \\( q(x) \\) are related by the same roots), the only polynomial that satisfies this is the zero polynomial. Therefore, the remainder is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:804"}
